  "transcript": "# Tourism\n\n## How holiday habits have shifted\n> British holidays are shorter and cheaper, driven by cost pressures, growth of short‑term rentals and demand for shareable experiences.\n\nChair: Do tourists' expectations and priorities in the UK look different than before?\nChris Haslam: Yes. Average holiday length has fallen from a 14‑night model decades ago to about 9.6 days overall, and even short breaks have shortened—3.3 days down to 2.9 in some cases.\nChris Haslam: People are 'trading down': either shorter stays or cheaper places. Since 2021 expenditure and duration have fallen and many guests now choose STRs—Airbnb‑type lets—now about 20–25% of the market while hotels are 40–45%.\nVicky Philpott: Families now look for new experiences they can share on social media—activities, cool cafes, child‑friendly things—not just lying on a beach.\nVicky Philpott: Cost matters: people self‑cater more, use travel‑hack tips to save, and try to spend on experiences rather than pricey hotel extras.\n\n\n## Coastal towns, the visitor experience and environmental worries\n> Seaside towns face falling overnight stays, high costs, seasonal pressure and growing public concern about water quality.\n\nChair: Are UK coastal destinations adapting to these changes?\nChris Haslam: Not really. Overnight stays at the British seaside have dropped 18% in ten years. Many resorts were built for a short Victorian season and need money to maintain big buildings and services—money they don't have.\nMr Bayo Alaba: What practical things can towns like Southend do to attract more visitors?\nChris Haslam: Two models work: (1) external investment and grant capture, like Great Yarmouth using renewables income and heritage grants; (2) community‑led renewal, like Bude, with local engagement and initiatives such as beach cleans and a 'coastal parliament'.\nVicky Philpott: Events pull people in—music, food festivals, weekend activations make places feel alive and bring return visits.\nDr Rupa Huq: Are the seaside towns still viable as conference or event venues?\nChris Haslam: Large conferences now go to cities because they can handle big crowds, security and accommodation in one place; many coastal towns lack the capacity.\nVicky Philpott: A major issue is sewage in bathing water. People see app alerts and toilet paper on beaches; it puts them off visiting the coast.\nChris Haslam: BEACH ratings are often historic; what matters are CSO discharges that can happen anytime. People rely on third‑party apps (Surfers Against Sewage etc.) to know real‑time water quality — that harms confidence in the British coast.\n\n\n## Pricing, levies and how to manage visitor numbers\n> High UK prices make many people prefer cheap, all‑inclusive holidays abroad; visitor levies must be designed carefully if used.\n\nMr Bayo Alaba: Why do many UK residents go abroad rather than holiday at home?\nChris Haslam: Value for money is the main driver. A mid‑range Cornwall hotel break can be far more expensive than an all‑inclusive Spanish week, even after adding food costs.\nVicky Philpott: School holiday pricing spikes make domestic breaks prohibitively expensive for many families.\nNigel Huddleston: What about an overnight visitor levy to manage overtourism and raise funds?\nChris Haslam: Small charges, like £5, won't deter tourists; they'd need to be significant to change behaviour, but that risks pushing visitors elsewhere. Another option is to develop UK as a lower‑volume, higher‑spend destination.\nAlex Polizzi: I'm against levies for places like Cornwall because domestic visitors are often lower‑income; extra charges could deter them and harm local small businesses.\nJamie Christon: We're pragmatic: an overnight levy could be useful if locally led, simple to administer and ring‑fenced for tourism, but we recognise local opposition.\n\n\n## Workforce, regulation and practical fixes\n> Tourism and attractions need help with rising costs, staff recruitment, training and clearer government backing; some specific, implementable fixes were suggested.\n\nChair: What is the health of the sector and what would make the biggest difference?\nAlex Polizzi: Costs have shot up: business rates, utilities, NI and minimum wage increases added nearly £950,000 to my three hotels' costs since Feb 2024. Staff costs can be nearly half of turnover.\nJamie Christon: At Chester Zoo we employ 1,200 people. The national insurance rise cost us £600,000 overnight. We need public support; local funding for destination marketing has fallen to near zero in many areas.\nMr Bayo Alaba: What blocks recruiting and keeping staff?\nAlex Polizzi: Transport in rural areas is poor; staff need cars or family taxis. Housing and staff accommodation are expensive; allowances staff can keep are small; and parental perceptions make hospitality a 'last choice' career.\nJamie Christon: We run apprenticeships and many staff stay, but chefs and night‑shift roles are hard to fill. Training is largely funded by employers; Government training support is limited.\nDr Rupa Huq: Should the industry have a stronger single voice in Government?\nAlex Polizzi: Yes. We lack a properly funded, professional centre of excellence for hospitality training and a visible ministerial champion who understands the sector's realities.\nJamie Christon: Zoos also fall between Departments: DEFRA regulates animals, but we are tourism, education and science. We'd like clearer departmental ownership so funds and policy fit our mixed role.\nJeff Smith: Any simple tech or digital fixes that would help attractions?\nJamie Christon: Yes — weather apps. Many visitors check a single weather icon and change plans. If major apps showed several icons across the day (morning, lunch, afternoon, evening) it would reduce lost visits — we estimate a bad icon day can cost us six figures.\nChris Haslam: Also: better, visible domestic promotion. Spain’s tourist board successfully promoted regions to Spaniards; the UK needs stronger, consistent campaigns so people remember what’s on their doorstep.\nJamie Christon: And a change to gift‑aid rules: currently attractions can only claim if a visitor donates at least 10% on top of a ticket; removing that threshold could unlock about £1m a year for our zoo.\nVicky Philpott: Finally, real‑time clarity on sewer discharges and stricter action on bathing water would restore public confidence in the coast.\nChair: If you each could ask Government for one change over five years, what would it be?\nJamie Christon: Put zoos and similar attractions into a single, coherent government framework so policy, funding and regulation match what we actually do (conservation, education, tourism).\nAlex Polizzi: Create and back a professional centre of excellence for hospitality to professionalise training, reassure parents and provide a recognised career pathway.\nChris Haslam: Invest in stronger domestic marketing, and tackle bathing water quality to stop the reputation damage that's driving people away from the coast.\nVicky Philpott: Make domestic holidays affordable for families—school‑term pricing spikes and the ability to get a decent, affordable family break matter hugely.\nChair: Thank you all — practical suggestions, clear problem areas and specific asks for Government to consider.\n",
